# 10. Topology of the Reals

An open set of real numbers is one that contains, along with each of its points, all points
that are near enough to it. In the hyperreal line "near enough" has a sharp meaning: infinitely
close. This chapter rewrites the elementary topology of the real line in those terms. Each
standard notion, which is defined with a quantifier over positive radii or over open covers,
becomes a statement about the halo of a real point and the enlargement $`{}^*A` of a set
$`A \subseteq \mathbb R`.

* A real number $`r` is interior to $`A` exactly when its halo lies inside $`{}^*A`; it is a
  closure point exactly when its halo meets $`{}^*A`; it is a limit point exactly when its halo
  meets $`{}^*A` in a point other than $`r`.
* A set is open when the enlargement contains the halo of each of its points, and closed when
  the shadow of every limited point of the enlargement belongs to the set.
* A set $`B` is compact exactly when every point of $`{}^*B` is infinitely close to a point of
  $`B`. This is Robinson's criterion; it turns arguments about open covers into arguments about
  single hyperreal points, and the Heine–Borel theorem, the preservation of compactness by
  continuous maps and the uniform continuity of continuous functions on compact sets all become
  short.

Every characterisation is linked to Mathlib's notion ({name}`interior`, {name}`closure`,
{name}`derivedSet`, {name}`IsOpen`, {name}`IsClosed`, {name}`IsCompact`,
{name}`UniformContinuousOn`), so the nonstandard criteria can be used directly on statements
about the real line phrased in Mathlib's language.

The last section looks at the hyperreal line itself, where there is no single best notion of
open set. Three candidates are compared: unions of intervals of real radius, which do not form a
topology; Robinson's S-topology, generated by intervals from which the points infinitely close
to the end points have been removed; and the interval topology, which is the order topology of
the hyperreal line.

Two statements of Section 10.5 are false as printed and are formalized in corrected form, with
the counterexamples proved: the intersection of the real-open sets containing a point is the
point alone and not its halo, and only intervals of limited length are intersections of two
intervals of real radius. The docstrings of the declarations concerned give the details.

## Sections

The chapter is split into two parts, each on its own page.

* 10.1 Interior, Closure, and Limit Points (part 1).
* 10.2 Open and Closed Sets (part 1).
* 10.3 Compactness (part 1).
* 10.4 Compactness and (Uniform) Continuity (part 1).
* 10.5 Topologies on the Hyperreals (part 2).

## Conventions and deviations

* Interior, closure and limit points are defined with real radii as in the book
  ({name}`Goldblatt.IsInteriorPoint`, {name}`Goldblatt.IsClosurePoint`,
  {name}`Goldblatt.IsLimitPoint`) and identified with Mathlib's notions. Open, closed and
  compact sets are Mathlib's {name}`IsOpen`, {name}`IsClosed` and {name}`IsCompact`; the
  book's definitions of the first two are theorems
  ({name}`Goldblatt.isOpen_iff_forall_isInteriorPoint`,
  {name}`Goldblatt.isClosed_iff_forall_isClosurePoint`), and the book's definition of
  compactness by open covers is Mathlib's {name}`isCompact_iff_finite_subcover`.
* In Theorem 10.1.1 the book proves part (2) by transfer and part (3) from part (2). Here
  part (3) is proved from part (1) by complements and part (2) from part (3).
* The condition in Robinson's criterion has its own name,
  {name}`Goldblatt.RobinsonCompact`. The book's five observations in Section 10.3 and its
  proofs of Theorems 10.3.1, 10.4.1 and 10.4.2 are carried out for this condition by
  reasoning about hyperreal points, and then restated for {name}`IsCompact` through the
  criterion {name}`Goldblatt.isCompact_iff_robinsonCompact`. Statements that duplicate a
  Mathlib theorem carry the suffix {lit}`by_halos`.
* In the proof of Robinson's criterion the reduction to a countable cover uses Mathlib's
  theorem that the real line is second countable, where the book argues with intervals having
  rational end points. Where the book says "by transfer", the hyperreal witness is given as
  the class of a sequence, as in Chapters 6 and 7.
* Sequences of sets indexed by positive integers in the book are indexed from zero, with
  $`\frac1{n+1}` in place of $`\frac1n` (Exercises 10.2.3 and 10.5.2).
* In Exercise 10.5.2 the intervals are taken to be nonempty, as the word interval suggests.
  Part (ii) needs only one nonempty interval and no bound on the lengths
  ({name}`Goldblatt.exists_mem_starSet_infClose_notMem_of_exists_lt`); part (iii) needs
  infinitely many nonempty intervals. If every interval is empty the enlargement is empty and
  so both real-open and S-open.
* Section 10.5 asserts that the intersection of the open sets containing a point is the halo
  of the point both for the real-open and for the S-open sets. This holds for S-open sets
  ({name}`Goldblatt.iInter_isSOpen_eq_hal`) but not for real-open sets, where the intersection
  is the point alone ({name}`Goldblatt.iInter_isRealOpen_eq_singleton`). What is true for
  real radii is that the neighbourhoods centred at the point intersect in its halo
  ({name}`Goldblatt.iInter_realNhd_eq_hal`).
* Section 10.5 asserts that every hyperreal interval is the intersection of two real-radius
  neighbourhoods. This holds for intervals of limited length
  ({name}`Goldblatt.Ioo_eq_realNhd_inter_realNhd`) and fails for intervals of unlimited length
  ({name}`Goldblatt.Ioo_zero_omega_ne_realNhd_inter_realNhd`). The conclusion the book draws
  from it, that a topology containing the real-radius neighbourhoods contains every
  interval-open set, is true and is proved by covering an interval with subintervals of length
  at most $`2` ({name}`Goldblatt.isOpen_of_isIntervalOpen_of_realNhd`).
* The book describes membership of an S-neighbourhood in two ways, by a shadow
  ({name}`Goldblatt.mem_sNeighbourhood_iff`) and by an appreciable difference
  ({name}`Goldblatt.mem_sNeighbourhood_iff_appreciable`); in the second the difference must
  also be positive, which the book leaves implicit.
* The failure of real-open sets to be closed under intersection is proved in the book's
  generality ({name}`Goldblatt.not_isRealOpen_realNhd_inter_of_infClose`) and then for a
  specific pair of neighbourhoods ({name}`Goldblatt.exists_realNhd_inter_not_isRealOpen`).
* Two statements go slightly beyond the book: the interval topology is identified with the
  order topology of the hyperreal line
  ({name}`Goldblatt.intervalTopology_eq_orderTopology`), and the S-topology is shown not to
  be Hausdorff ({name}`Goldblatt.not_t2Space_sTopology`).
* Chapter 11 restates the three notions of Section 10.5 in pointwise form in the namespace
  {lit}`HyperTopology`; those are the right-hand sides of
  {name}`Goldblatt.isRealOpen_iff`, {name}`Goldblatt.isSOpen_iff` and
  {name}`Goldblatt.isIntervalOpen_iff`.

